    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-132582",
      "knowledge_point_id": "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point",
      "problem": "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=\\pi .$ Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $1$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(x+y){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$",
      "worked_solution": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi .$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $x=1$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,\\pi ).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\pi ) & =(1+\\pi ){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =(1+\\pi ){e}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\pi }{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{\\pi }{{e}^{2}}"
