    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-71224",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point",
      "problem": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{2}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$",
      "worked_solution": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & ={f}_{y}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)(y-0) \\\\ z-2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-\\frac{1}{2}y+2$ (or $y+2z-4=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,2⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,-2,1⟩.$",
      "answer_fields": [
